    {
      "id": "fence",
      "chapter": "The fence",
      "narration": "Here's why. The latch has a finger underneath, called the fence, that sits just above the edges of the discs. A small spring holds the latch up, in a notch on the shackle. In many padlocks, pulling the shackle drives a ramp that pushes the latch down toward the discs. But if even one disc's edge is in the way, the fence can't drop, the latch can't move, and the shackle stays locked.",
